{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794269335,"nonce":"cB8jQsPKIfb263rQuZS8D4vse9E=","title":"incremental_update resets the taffy styles of every node; after a VisualOnly update the next relayout drops all CSS layout rules","message":"Symptom (Calendar, hand test by the owner): pressing and holding the mouse on a day cell makes the sidebar vanish right to left and the header jump; releasing restores it. Reproduced in the live probe (kwin --virtual, real input, fs-calendar 0.0.2).\n\nCause, with evidence:\n- RenderTree::update_render_props_in_place(_boxed) calls layout_tree.set_style(node, element.layout_style()) for every node. That style is the builder's raw style; the stylesheet's layout rules (width, padding, gap, flex-direction, ...) were written onto the taffy style earlier by apply_stylesheet_layout_overrides and are gone after the call.\n- The VisualOnly branch of the runners neither re-applies the layout rules nor lays out. The next compute_layout comes from the state-style pass (hover or press changes the router fingerprint, apply_stylesheet_state_styles reports a change, compute_layout runs, frame/present.rs), and it lays out the raw styles.\n- Probe log of the Calendar around the first click: REB result=VisualOnly, then STATE changed=true with LAYOUT_BEFORE header row 1280x44 with children at (18,14) and (177,5); LAYOUT_AFTER header row 385x44 with children at (0,0) 154x44 and (172,0), body at x=385, status bar at x=1665 (outside the 1280 window). The layout stays broken while the button is held; the release causes a rebuild (LayoutChanged, rules re-applied) and the picture is right again.\n- Headless reproducer (blinc_layout, no window): tree with .wide { width: 123px }, apply_all_stylesheet_styles, compute_layout (width 123), incremental_update with a colour change (VisualOnly), compute_layout again: width 0.\n\nFix: update_render_props_in_place(_boxed) leaves the taffy style of a node alone when the node's own hash (it covers the layout style) is unchanged, and the nodes it replaced get the stylesheet's layout rules back in restyle_replaced_nodes (apply_stylesheet_layout_overrides_filtered over exactly those nodes). The LayoutChanged branch then needs no whole-tree layout-rule pass either.\n\nSame root as the class-style loss of issue 70 / PR 71 (props replaced by the new element's props), here for the layout half.","files":null}]}